Title: The Last Signal
Author: Kokoro Test

FADE IN:

EXT. ROOFTOP OBSERVATORY - NIGHT

A group of researchers huddle around a glowing terminal.
The city sprawls below them, indifferent.

NARRATOR
It began like any other Tuesday. No one expected the signal to arrive at 11:47 PM, and no one expected what came next.

DR. CHEN
The waveform is unlike anything in our database. Forty years in this field and I have never seen a pattern repeat at this frequency.

ALICE
Could it be natural? Some kind of pulsar we haven't catalogued yet?

DR. CHEN
That was my first thought. But look at the intervals. They are prime numbers, Alice. All of them.

MARCO
Wait wait wait. Prime numbers? Like, someone chose them? Are we actually doing this right now?

SOPHIA
Marcus, do compose yourself. Extraordinary claims aside, we should verify the source coordinates before drawing any conclusions.

MARCO
It's Marco. And I'm completely composed. I'm just — this is enormous!

ALICE
Sophia's right. We need a second source to confirm. If this is a reflection or interference from the satellite array—

THE STRANGER
It isn't.

(everyone turns)

SOPHIA
I beg your pardon. Who let you onto this roof?

THE STRANGER
You've been expecting this signal for eleven years. Your grant proposals said as much. The question you should be asking is why tonight.

DR. CHEN
How do you know about our grant proposals?

EMMA
(rushing in, breathless)
Sorry I'm late, the elevator was — oh. Who's that?

MARCO
That's what we're trying to figure out.

THE STRANGER
You have approximately four minutes before the window closes. I suggest you start recording.

NARRATOR
Dr. Chen reached for the terminal without another word. Some instincts outlast skepticism.

ALICE
Four minutes. Okay. Recording. What do we do after?

THE STRANGER
After — you decide what the world is ready to hear.

EMMA
That is either the most exciting or the most terrifying thing anyone has ever said to me.

MARCO
Why not both?

SOPHIA
Both. Definitively both.

NARRATOR
The signal lasted three minutes and fifty-eight seconds. What they found inside it would take the rest of their lives to understand. But that night, on a rooftop above a city that had no idea, they listened.

FADE OUT.
